<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>The Connection between Sequences and Terms</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01118</md:content-id>
    <md:title>The Connection between Sequences and Terms</md:title>
    <md:uuid>952dc309-bbb5-41dd-82f4-3933113af5a7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <media alt="An interactive activity to further practice mathematical skills. Produced by Geogrebra.">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://openstax.org/r/checkers"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00001">Some checkers are lined up, with blue on one side, red on the other, and 1 empty space between them. A <emphasis>move</emphasis> in this checker game pushes any checker forward 1 space, or jumps over any 1 checker of the other color. Jumping the same color is not allowed, moving backward is not allowed, and 2 checkers cannot occupy the same space.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="A row of six circles: the first three circles are blue, a gap, then the last three circles are red. All circles are outlined in black and set against a green background.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_17_06_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00003">You complete the puzzle by switching the colors completely: ending up with black on the right, red on the left, and 1 empty space between them.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">1. </span>Using 1 checker on each side, complete the puzzle. What is the smallest number of moves needed?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00006">3 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">2. </span>Using 3 checkers on each side, complete the puzzle. What is the smallest number of moves needed?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00009">15 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">3. </span>Estimate the number of moves needed if there are 2 or 4 checkers on each side. Then test your guesses.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00012">8 moves and 24 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00013" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">4. </span>Noah says he used the solution for 3 checkers on each side to help him solve the puzzle for 4 checkers. Describe how this might happen.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00015">The moves that don’t involve the fourth checker on each side are the same as before, so it is possible to decide the number of moves by running the first half of the three-checker game, then moving the fourth checkers, and then running the second half.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00016" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">5. </span>How many moves do you think it will take to complete a puzzle with 7 checkers on each side?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00018">63 moves</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
        <para id="para-00019"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_4_14_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise> 
    </note>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Additional Resources</title>
        
  <section id="sect-00003"><title>Using a Table to Find Missing Values</title>

    <table class="unnumbered os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002" summary="This table presents the relationship between the number of checkers and the required moves, including a missing value.">
      <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>Number of Checkers</emphasis></entry><entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            4
          </entry>
          <entry>
            7
          </entry>
          <entry>
            8
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>Number of Moves Needed</emphasis></entry><entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            8
          </entry>
          <entry>
            15
          </entry>
          <entry>
            24
          </entry>
          <entry>
            63
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ?
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para id="para-00021">What is the pattern that you used in the previous activity to find the number of moves depending on the number of
      checkers?</para>
    <para id="para-00022">Each row is multiplied by 1 more, so:</para>
    <para id="para-00023"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00024"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00025"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00026"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>24</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00027">How many moves are needed with 5 checkers?</para>
    <para id="para-00028"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>35</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> moves</para>
    <para id="para-00029">What about 6 checkers?</para>
    <para id="para-00030"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>48</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> moves</para>
    <note class="try" id="note-00002">
    <title>Using a Table to Find Missing Values</title>
    <exercise id="exer-00006">
      <problem id="prob-00006">
        
        <para id="para-00031">Using the table and the pattern, how many moves are needed with 8 checkers?</para>
      
      <para id="para-00032"/>
      
      </problem><solution id="sol-00006">
      <para id="para-00033">Here is how to solve this problem using a general strategy:</para>
      
      <para id="para-00034">The next column in the table had 7 checkers and then multiplied 7 by 9 to get 63 moves.</para>
      
      <para id="para-00035">Next, the 8 would need to be multiplied by 10, so:</para>
      
      <para id="para-00036"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>80</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00037">80 moves are needed.</para>
    
    </solution></exercise></note>
    </section>
      </section>
      
  </content>
